<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-dirac-delta-lti-systems" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Dirac Delta Distribution and LTI systems</title>



    <definition xml:id="def-dirac-delta" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
      <title>Sifting Property of the Dirac Distribution</title>
      <idx>sifting property</idx>

      <notation>
        <usage><m>\delta(t)</m></usage>
        <description>The Dirac delta function</description>
      </notation>

      <statement>
        <p>
            The <term>Dirac delta distribution</term>, denoted <m>\delta(t)</m>, is an idealized impulse concentrated
            at instant in time. Although it is commonly referred to as a function, it is more properly understood as a
            <term>distribution</term> (generalized function) whose defining property is the <term>sifting property</term>:
            <md>
                \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \delta(t-a) f(a) \, dt = f(a) \, ,
            </md>
            where <m>f(t)</m> is assumed to be a sufficiently well-behaved function.
        </p>

      </statement>
    </definition>

    <remark>
        <p>
            Intuitively, <m>\delta(t-a)</m> extracts the value of a signal at time <m>t=a</m>. The delta distribution
            as described in <xref ref="note-dirac-delta-interpretation"/> has a total area,
            <md>
                \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \delta(t-a) \, dt = 1 \, .
            </md>

            This highlights the fact that the Dirac delta is more appropriately labelled a <term> distribution</term> rather than a function.
            Specifically, its meaning is determined via its behavior under integration rather than its value at a given point.
        </p>

    </remark>

    <definition>
        <title>Impulse response</title>
        <idx>impulse response</idx>
        <notation>
            <usage><m>h(t)</m></usage>
            <description>The impulse response of an LTI system to some input</description>
        </notation>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In the context of LTI systems, the Dirac delta plays a fundamental role in the definition of an idealized
                response of a system to some impulse input. Specifically, if if an LTI system is excited by <m>\delta(t)</m>,
                the resulting output is called the <term>impulse response</term>, <m>h(t)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

    </definition>

    <remark xml:id="rem-dirac-delta-continuous-superposition">
      <p>
       Any signal <m>x(t)</m> can be represented as a continuous superposition of shifted impulses,
         <md>
           x(t) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \delta(t-\tau) x(\tau) \, d\tau \, .
         </md>
      </p>
    </remark>


 </subsubsection>